<section class="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>How many square feet does one gallon of paint cover?</h3>
    <p>One gallon of standard interior paint covers roughly 350 to 400 square feet on smooth, primed walls. Textured walls or unprimed drywall absorb more paint, reducing coverage closer to 250 to 300 square feet per gallon.</p>

    <h3>Do I need primer before painting interior walls?</h3>
    <p>Primer is recommended when painting bare drywall, making drastic color shifts (such as dark to light), or covering water stains. Paint-and-primer combinations work well for repainting previously painted walls of similar tones.</p>

    <h3>How do doors and windows affect paint volume?</h3>
    <p>A standard interior door accounts for approximately 21 square feet, while a standard window subtracts 15 square feet of non-painted wall space. Factoring in openings prevents buying unnecessary paint.</p>

    <h3>How much extra paint should I buy for touch ups?</h3>
    <p>Purchasing an extra 10 percent allowance or keeping one quart of leftover paint ensures exact color matches for future scratch repairs and wall patches.</p>
